     "title": "Practice: Trace LIGO Events This Week",
     "content": "**Goal**: Connect modern black-hole astronomy to your daily news feed.\n\n1. **Visit gw-openscience.org**. LIGO publishes alerts about gravitational-wave detections in real time. Browse the **GWTC catalog** of confirmed events.\n\n2. **Pick one event from 2024 or 2025**. Note: source masses, distance, signal duration. Was it BH-BH? BH-NS? NS-NS?\n\n3. **Open the strain data** — LIGO's actual interferometer signal. See the **chirp** shape with your own eyes.\n\n4. **Find the corresponding paper** on arXiv. Read the abstract. Cosmology is now an everyday science — papers are open-access.\n\n5. **Watch Sagan's 'Cosmos' episode 9 again** (The Lives of the Stars). His explanation of stellar death describes the physical setup for exactly the kinds of mergers LIGO now sees.\n\n6. **Tell someone**: \"I read about a real black hole merger this week.\" Make modern astronomy part of your social vocabulary. Sagan's vision was a public scientific worldview — embodied in your conversations.\n\n**Bonus**: download **Stellarium**, locate the **galactic center** (Sagittarius constellation). You're looking at our home supermassive black hole.",
