     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Extremophiles: Life's Surprising Range",
     "content": "What conditions can life tolerate? **Far more extreme than 1970s biology imagined**.\n\n**Extremophiles** (organisms thriving in extreme conditions) have been found at every supposed limit:\n\n• **Hyperthermophiles** (*Pyrolobus fumarii*): grow at **113 °C** — boiling water plus 13 degrees\n\n• **Psychrophiles** (Antarctic microbes): active at **−20 °C** in liquid brine within ice\n\n• **Acidophiles** (*Picrophilus oshimae*): grow at **pH 0** — battery acid\n\n• **Alkaliphiles**: thrive at **pH 12** (oven cleaner)\n\n• **Halophiles** (*Haloarcula*): in saturated salt — Dead Sea, salt mines\n\n• **Barophiles**: under **1,000+ atmospheres** of pressure (deep ocean trenches)\n\n• **Radioresistant** (*Deinococcus radiodurans*): survives **5,000 Gray** of radiation (1,000× the lethal dose for humans)\n\n• **Desiccation-resistant** (*tardigrades*): survive **complete vacuum**, **0 K (cryptobiosis)**, decades without water\n\n**Implications**:\n• Conditions on **Mars** (cold, dry, salty, radioactive) are tolerable to known life\n• **Europa's ocean** (cold, salty, dark) is within tolerance\n• **Enceladus's plume conditions** are tolerable\n• Subsurface life on **Mars** could persist today\n\nIn 2024, the **Perseverance** rover detected organic molecules in a Martian rock. We are sampling for potential return as you read this.\n\n**Life is more robust than we thought**. The window of habitability is **wider** than the Sun-Earth case.",
