     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Panspermia: Life From Space?",
     "content": "**Panspermia** is the hypothesis that **life did not originate on Earth** but came from space — either as molecules, spores, or simple organisms.\n\n**Lithopanspermia** (rocks carry life between planets):\n• Asteroid impacts blast rocks off planets at escape velocity\n• Some rocks reach other worlds\n• **Microbes inside** could survive the journey\n• Examples: **Martian meteorites** like ALH84001 (debated 1996 \"fossilized microbes\" claim, now contested but discussion continues)\n\n**Directed panspermia** (intelligent beings seeded life):\n• Proposed by **Francis Crick and Leslie Orgel** (1973) — partly tongue-in-cheek\n• Sagan thought it was unlikely but possible\n• Not falsifiable in detail\n\n**Pseudopanspermia** (molecules from space):\n• **Murchison meteorite** (1969 Australia) contains **80+ amino acids**, many extraterrestrial\n• **Comets** contain glycine, ethanolamine, urea, and complex organics\n• Interstellar molecular clouds contain methanol, formaldehyde, simple sugars\n• In 2022, **Hayabusa2** brought back **Ryugu asteroid samples** containing amino acids — confirming organic chemistry on small bodies\n\n**The key insight**: even if Earth's life didn't come from space, **the building blocks of life are made everywhere in the universe**. Origin of life is not Earth-specific chemistry.\n\nThis matters for astrobiology: **life is the natural endpoint of chemistry plus time plus suitable conditions**. The universe should be rich with the right starting materials.",
