     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: Adopt One Open Question for Life",
     "content": "**Goal**: pick one cosmological mystery and commit to following it for the rest of your life.\n\n**Step 1**: Choose ONE from this list (or another that matters to you):\n• The Hubble Tension\n• The nature of dark matter\n• The nature of dark energy\n• The Origin of Life\n• Biosignatures on K2-18 b or TRAPPIST-1\n• Confirmation of cosmic inflation (B-modes)\n• First neutrino background detection\n• Proof or refutation of the multiverse\n• Resolution of the information paradox in black holes\n• First direct detection of a primordial black hole\n\n**Step 2**: Pick three resources to follow it through:\n• An arXiv tag (e.g., astro-ph.CO)\n• A trusted reviewer (a science writer or podcast: Quanta Magazine, Sean Carroll's Mindscape, PBS Space Time)\n• A specific lab or project\n\n**Step 3**: Set up notification:\n• Google Scholar alert on the topic\n• arXiv daily email subscription\n• Calendar reminder to check in every 6 months\n\n**Step 4**: Write a one-page \"current state\" summary in your own words. Each year, rewrite it. Watch your understanding deepen.\n\n**Step 5**: When the question is **decisively answered** (or when you die), reflect: did the answer surprise you? Did it change your worldview? Did the journey matter?\n\nSagan committed his life to SETI knowing he might never hear a signal. **The commitment itself was the point**. Choose your question. Begin.",
